Data   : the web page reads NO API — it renders an inline hardcoded `roadmapItems` array. This port mirrors that
         exactly: RoadmapPageModel.kt declares the static `roadmapCatalog` (17 entries: 5 done, 1 current, 2 next,
         9 future — verbatim from the web), and RoadmapPageViewModel projects buildRoadmapSnapshot(catalog) through
         the shared cache-then-network Resource -> UiState pipeline (Resource.Success) so the stateless screen
         renders the same loading/empty/success matrix every parity page uses. No HTTP, no store. refresh()/retry()
         re-derive. buildRoadmapSnapshot reproduces the web's two derivations: the four-phase progress tally (web
         maps over EVERY phase, including empties) and the per-phase card sections (web drops a phase whose
         `items.length === 0`).
Strings: the web page i18n surface is only roadmap.title + roadmap.subtitle (already generated into the catalog).
         The phase labels are web `t('roadmap.phase.<p>', '<fallback>')` references whose keys are NOT in the web
         en.json (they rely on the inline fallback), and the item titles/descriptions/feature bullets are HARDCODED
         English literals in the web TSX (not i18n keys at all). To honor ADR-014 (zero hardcoded literals) the
         native port externalizes EVERY visible literal into the platform string catalog under the web `roadmap.*`
         namespace — 163 new keys added directly to res/values{,-ar,-he}/strings.xml. This follows the committed
         sibling precedent (SharedDrivePage added translation_share_expired_* — t()-fallback keys not present in
         web en.json — directly to the generated strings.xml). The two manifest-tracked keys (roadmap.title,
         roadmap.subtitle) are reused verbatim; phase-label values ("Completed"/"In Progress"/"Up Next"/"Future")
         match the web `t()` fallbacks. Documented here, not silent (Covenant #9). ar/he placeholder locales get the
         same English text (their existing roadmap.title/subtitle are English fallback too). Every literal resolves
         via stringResource(R.string.translation_*).
Color  : the web assigns each phase a literal hex for its dot/label/icon/section-header but a separate Badge
         `variant` (success/info/warning/danger for done/current/next/future). Material 3 forbids hardcoded colors
         (ADR-005) and the token palette (TeslaTokens.status) exposes exactly those four semantic status colors, so
         the port resolves ALL of a phase's coloring from the single status token matching its web Badge variant —
         internally consistent per phase and dynamic-color/dark-theme aware (RoadmapPage.kt phaseColor/phaseVariant).
Nav    : Destinations 'roadmap' -> /roadmap pre-existing (Destinations.kt:170; metadata-only count unchanged); host
         wired via PageHosts.register(roadmap){ RoadmapRoute() }; deep link auto-registered by TeslaSyncNavHost.

